# Collatz (hailstone) sequences
#
# Adapted from the Logtalk example collection.
#
# Halve an even number, treble an odd one and add one, repeat. Every start
# value tried so far eventually reaches 1; nobody knows whether all of them
# do. How many steps does each of the first 27 numbers take?
#
# The chain rules invent their own values through arithmetic rather than
# drawing them from a declared range, so nothing here bounds how far the
# sequence may climb: 27 reaches 9232 before it comes back down. That also
# means termination rests on the conjecture: a seed whose sequence never
# reached 1 would not give a finite least fixed point.

seed(N) :- N in [1 .. 27].

# chain(Seed, Value, K): starting from Seed, Value is reached after K steps.
chain(N, N, 0) :- seed(N).
chain(N, M, K) :- chain(N, P, K0), P > 1, P % 2 == 0, M = P / 2,     K = K0 + 1.
chain(N, M, K) :- chain(N, P, K0), P > 1, P % 2 == 1, M = 3 * P + 1, K = K0 + 1.

steps(N, K) :- chain(N, 1, K).

?- steps(Seed, Steps).

output predicate longest(max(K)) :- steps(_, K).
output predicate peak(max(M)) :- chain(_, M, _).
